<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kōraku-en</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1700</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Korakuen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kōraku-en is a landscape garden in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/okayama">Okayama</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, built for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ikeda-tsunamasa">Ikeda Tsunamasa</a> of the Okayama Domain and completed in 1700. A stroll garden of lawns, ponds, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> houses and borrowed views of Okayama Castle, it was opened to the public in 1884. It is ranked as one of the Three Great Gardens of Japan.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/koraku-en.jpg" alt="Kōraku-en" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 663highland · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:250505_Korakuen_Okayama_Japan06s3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
